# 4. REDESIGN THE GLOBAL COLOUR SYSTEM

Audit the current BrainCode colour palette and create a consistent global colour system.

Use a professional neutral foundation.

Preferred foundation:

- White and subtle warm-neutral surfaces.
- Light neutral backgrounds where appropriate.
- Dark charcoal primary text.
- Medium gray secondary text.
- Muted gray supporting text.
- Subtle neutral dividers.
- Restrained brand accent colours.

Do not introduce unnecessary new brand colours.

Preserve the existing BrainCode identity while refining its application.

## Create reusable colour tokens

Define consistent tokens for:

- Primary background.
- Secondary background.
- Surface.
- Elevated surface.
- Primary text.
- Secondary text.
- Muted text.
- Border.
- Divider.
- Primary brand colour.
- Primary hover state.
- Focus state.
- Success.
- Warning.
- Error.
- Information.
- Disabled state.

### Colour application rules

- Primary buttons should use the consistent brand colour.
- Secondary buttons should use neutral styling.
- Destructive actions should use danger styling.
- Success states should use success styling.
- Warning messages should use warning styling.
- Information messages should use information styling.
- Disabled elements should look disabled without becoming unreadable.

Avoid giving every section its own colour.

Avoid placing a coloured background behind every group of content.

Use colour to communicate meaning and hierarchy.

## Border and outline refinement

The existing coloured box outlines make parts of the application look artificial.

Reduce unnecessary outlines.

Use borders only where they provide useful separation.

Examples:

- Input fields.
- Selected navigation items.
- Code editor boundaries.
- Functional workspace panels.
- Table separators when needed.
- Dialog boundaries.
- Meaningful content divisions.

Use subtle, consistent neutral borders.

Do not use a different border colour for every card.

Do not place borders around every paragraph, lesson section, or list.

