# 7. COMPLETE RESPONSIVE DESIGN REFINEMENT

This is one of the highest-priority parts of Phase 26.

The current interface may resize technically, but some layouts are not visually balanced or properly organised on smaller screens.

Do not simply shrink desktop layouts.

Design responsive behaviour intentionally.

## Desktop targets

Test:

- 1440 × 900.
- 1280 × 800.
- 1024 × 768.

Desktop requirements:

- Use screen space intelligently.
- Maintain balanced sidebars and content.
- Avoid excessively wide reading areas.
- Avoid compressed main content.
- Use multi-column layouts where appropriate.
- Keep important content prominent.
- Avoid unnecessary empty space.
- Prevent overcrowded navigation.
- Keep controls aligned.

## Tablet targets

Test:

- 900 × 1000.
- 820 × 1180.
- 768 × 1024.

Tablet requirements:

- Adapt navigation appropriately.
- Reposition or collapse sidebars.
- Adjust column counts.
- Reorganise controls.
- Prevent content compression.
- Avoid overflow.
- Maintain consistent spacing.
- Avoid awkward desktop layouts that are only partially collapsed.

## Mobile targets

Test:

- 430 × 932.
- 414 × 896.
- 390 × 844.
- 375 × 812.
- 360 × 800.
- 320 × 700.

Mobile is not an afterthought.

Every major interface must be usable and visually balanced on mobile.

### Mobile acceptance requirements

Ensure:

- No unintended horizontal scrolling.
- No clipped content.
- No overlapping elements.
- No tiny buttons.
- No unnecessarily oversized controls.
- No awkwardly stretched cards.
- No extremely narrow text areas.
- No text squeezed between controls.
- No desktop sidebars consuming the screen.
- No overlapping navigation.
- No forms exceeding viewport width.
- No content hidden accidentally.
- No huge decorative elements wasting screen space.
- No excessive empty space.
- No inconsistent page padding.

### Mobile-specific layout behaviour

Desktop:

Header  
Title and actions  
Sidebar and main content

Mobile:

Header  
Title  
Actions  
Main content  
Sidebar available through an appropriate drawer or navigation control.

Desktop three-column layouts should become intelligently ordered mobile layouts.

Inline desktop filters should become compact mobile controls or expandable filter sections.

Tables should use the most appropriate mobile presentation:

- Responsive tables with intentional horizontal scrolling.
- Reorganised compact lists.
- Alternative structured data layouts.

Do not blindly convert every table into cards.

### Mobile spacing

Review and refine:

- Page padding.
- Section spacing.
- Heading spacing.
- Button spacing.
- Form spacing.
- Navigation spacing.
- Footer spacing.
- Text line lengths.
- Drawer dimensions.
- Toolbar spacing.

Mobile should feel compact, comfortable, and professionally arranged.

Do not copy desktop spacing directly onto mobile.

